        "content": "<p>Hi all,</p>\n<p>My first Lean project, Computable Analysis without completed reals (Far from complete, mostly autoformalized by Codex with GPT 5.5 Pro),</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/liuyao12/computable-analysis\">https://github.com/liuyao12/computable-analysis</a></p>\n<p>with an (AI-generated) blueprint:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://liuyao12.github.io/computable-analysis/\">https://liuyao12.github.io/computable-analysis/</a></p>\n<p>The basic idea is to define (computable) real and complex numbers from executable algorithms. A \"real number\" starts as a nested sequence of rational intervals with shrinking width; equivalence is by overlap. I wanted to see how much calculus (pre-19th century mathematics) can be done without the completeness of real numbers.</p>\n<p>This project started as an autoformalization experiment in Codex, <strong>deliberately without mathlib</strong> (except for rational numbers). It could get some definitions and propositions, but not very far in closing up nontrivial propositions. So I changed strategy: I asked Codex to draft a blueprint, given what I wanted, then hopefully Codex has a better chance at formalizing everything. The current project is therefore both auto-formalized and auto-blueprinted, with Sections 1.1-1.3 now marked as human-checked (that is, modified or rewritten by me).</p>\n<p>One motivation is educational: I would like this to become something first-year calculus students could play with, and get familiar with Lean. Instead of starting from abstract completeness, they can evaluate concrete algorithms for numbers/functions they already know, then prove that different algorithms define equivalent numbers/functions. Secondly, I hear that computing digits is not something that mathlib is good at, making certain arguments cumbersome. Would this be a helpful addition?</p>\n<p>Here's a snippet of what it does now (taken from <code>Pi.lean</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- The Leibniz alternating series, computing `pi / 4`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">leibnizSeries</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RealRaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">compute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List.range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">foldl</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term1</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hi</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term2</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hi</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lo</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hi</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Pi from the Leibniz alternating series. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">piLeibniz</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RealRaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">leibnizSeries</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Pi from Machin's formula. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">piMachin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RealRaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">arctan</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">arctan</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">239</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"kn\">namespace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PiExamples</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"bp\">!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">piLeibniz.compute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">display</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"bp\">!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">piMachin.compute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">display</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"bp\">!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">piCircleArea.compute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">display</span>\n<span class=\"k\">#eval</span><span class=\"bp\">!</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">piCircumference.compute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">display</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">end</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">PiExamples</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hovering over the <code>eval</code>s would display:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Text only\"><pre><span></span><code>\"[3.09..., 3.18...] width=0.09756\"\n\"[3.1415926535897932384626433832712..., 3.1415926535897932384626433832798...] width=8.581e-30\"\n\"[3.1415912..., 3.1415933...] width=0.000002077\"\n\"[3.1415923..., 3.1415933...] width=0.000001038\"\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>So the algorithms are not just specifications; they can be run. Then the proof side tries to show \"validity\" of these algorithms and their \"equivalences\".</p>\n<p>I am curious whether this could be a useful target for more specialized autoformalization/proof-automation tools. Any feedback is greatly appreciated.</p>",
